**Exceptions are entitlements.** An app that truly needs a relaxed rule asks for it with a signed entitlement, for example:

- `com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables`: honour DYLD variables again;
- `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation`: load plug-ins from other developers;
- `com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit` and `allow-unsigned-executable-memory`: writable and executable memory;
- `com.apple.security.get-task-allow`: let debuggers attach (notarization rejects it).

Apple's documentation says to use only the entitlements "absolutely necessary". For a defender, each one is a hole you can inventory: an app with `disable-library-validation` and a Full Disk Access grant is a ready-made host for injected code.

Malware that dies at reboot has to trick the user again. **Persistence** is how it comes back. On a Mac the most common way is to ask `launchd`, the process that starts everything else (process ID 1), to start it.

`launchd` reads **property lists** (plists, Apple's XML or binary key-value file format). The `launchd` manual lists where they live:

- `~/Library/LaunchAgents`: per-user agents provided by the user;
- `/Library/LaunchAgents`: per-user agents provided by the administrator;
- `/Library/LaunchDaemons`: system-wide daemons provided by the administrator;
- `/System/Library/LaunchAgents` and `/System/Library/LaunchDaemons`: Apple's, on the read-only system volume.

An **agent** runs in a user's login session (it can show UI and reach that user's files); a **daemon** runs system-wide, usually as root, with no user logged in. ATT&CK: [T1543.001](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/001/) Launch Agent and [T1543.004](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/004/) Launch Daemon.

