One Mac you can inspect by hand. Five thousand you query. **osquery**, an open source project that describes itself as "a SQL powered operating system instrumentation, monitoring, and analytics framework", turns OS state into tables you query with SQL. Its macOS tables map neatly onto this course; the column lists below come from the table definitions in [osquery's source](https://github.com/osquery/osquery/tree/master/specs/darwin):

- `launchd`: every LaunchAgent and LaunchDaemon plist in the default paths (`label`, `program`, `program_arguments`, `run_at_load`, `keep_alive`);
- `signature`: code signing status for a path (`signed`, `identifier`, `team_identifier`, `authority`, `entitlements`);
- `es_process_events`: process executions from Endpoint Security (`path`, `cmdline`, `parent`, `signing_id`, ...);
- `sip_config`, `gatekeeper`, `xprotect_entries`: control state;
- `unified_log`: the unified log, filtered with a `predicate`.
